<h2><strong>Understanding Florida’s Car Insurance Laws</strong></h2>
<p>Car insurance laws in Florida require drivers to have a minimum amount of liability insurance coverage. Here’s what is needed:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Personal Injury Protection (PIP):</strong> Florida is a “no-fault” state, meaning that in the event of an accident, each driver’s own insurance will cover their damages up to a certain point, regardless of who was at fault. Florida law requires all drivers to carry a minimum of $10,000 in PIP coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Property Damage Liability (PDL):</strong> This covers damages you may cause to someone else’s property (like their car) in an accident. Again, the minimum required by law is $10,000.</li>
</ol>
<p>Although these are the minimum requirements, it’s often recommended to consider higher limits for better protection. It’s also important to note that vehicles registered as taxis must carry bodily injury liability (BIL) coverage of $125,000 per person, $250,000 per occurrence and $50,000 for PDL coverage.</p>
